From 198eb480926dfee1093bb228b12df202289cb225 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 15:26:21 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 86/94] dt-bindings: opp: qcom-osm: Allow a peak bandwidth per
 OPP

The CPU OPPs of an OS-programmed OSM can carry the DDR bandwidth the
CPUs need at that frequency, the mainline form of the downstream
cpufreq-to-bandwidth map: qcom-cpufreq-hw already votes an OPP's
opp-peak-kBps on the CPU's interconnect paths when it switches to it.
Accept the property in the OPP nodes.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01SXjPGasJxD6XRYU1J9jsnW
---
 Documentation/devicetree/bindings/opp/opp-v2-qcom-osm.yaml | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/opp/opp-v2-qcom-osm.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/opp/opp-v2-qcom-osm.yaml
index 3da8cf616..e499b71c0 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/opp/opp-v2-qcom-osm.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/opp/opp-v2-qcom-osm.yaml
@@ -17,7 +17,8 @@ description:
   table is programmed by the OS, as on SDM630/SDM636/SDM660 where the
   boot firmware leaves it empty. Next to the frequency, every OPP carries
   the raw OSM fields of its look-up table entry, and its CPR corner through
-  required-opps.
+  required-opps. An OPP may also name the peak bandwidth the CPUs vote on
+  their interconnect paths while they run at that frequency.
 
 properties:
   compatible:
@@ -37,6 +38,8 @@ patternProperties:
 
       required-opps: true
 
+      opp-peak-kBps: true
+
       turbo-mode: true
 
       qcom,pll-override:
@@ -85,5 +88,6 @@ examples:
             qcom,pll-override = <0x3200020>;
             qcom,spare-data = <1>;
             qcom,pll-div = <1>;
+            opp-peak-kBps = <799014>;
         };
     };
